# Copilot Prompt — Unified Splunk Audit Logging Framework for .NET Microservices

## Role
You are a senior .NET software architect. Design and implement a shared, reusable audit-logging framework that multiple independent .NET solutions (built and deployed like microservices) will all reference, so every application writes audit events to a single centralized Splunk audit index in exactly the same shape — no per-team drift in field names, types, or formats.

## Background
- I maintain several separate .NET solutions/`.csproj` projects. Each is isolated — own repo/solution, own deployment pipeline, built and run independently (microservice-style), no shared runtime between them.
- Each solution currently implements its own "post action" step that writes audit information to Splunk. That logic was written independently per project, so the data landing in Splunk is inconsistent today — different field names, types, timestamp formats, missing fields — even though it's meant to represent the same kind of event.
- Goal: replace all of the bespoke, per-project implementations with one shared framework, published once and consumed identically everywhere, so audit data is queryable/joinable in Splunk without per-service cleanup.

## Objective
Produce a single class library (packaged as an internal NuGet package) that:
1. Defines one canonical, versioned audit-event schema — the single source of truth for "what an audit record looks like."
2. Ships a small, ergonomic API each consuming project calls from its own "post action" step.
3. Handles delivery to Splunk (HTTP Event Collector) reliably, without adding meaningful latency or becoming a new source of outages for the host app.
4. Makes it harder to do the wrong thing than the right thing: a team dropping this package in and calling `PublishAsync(...)` should get a compliant, consistent event with almost no way to accidentally diverge from the schema.

## Functional Requirements

### 1. Canonical event model
Immutable, strongly-typed `AuditEvent`. Treat this table as the contract — don't let consumers bolt on arbitrary top-level fields.

| Field | Type | Required | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `eventId` | `Guid` | Yes | Generated by the framework, not the caller |
| `schemaVersion` | `string` | Yes | e.g. `"1.0"` — set by the framework |
| `timestampUtc` | `DateTimeOffset` | Yes | Framework sets this (UTC, ISO-8601) — don't trust a caller-supplied time |
| `serviceName` | `string` | Yes | Bound once at startup from config, not passed per call |
| `environment` | `string` | Yes | Dev / QA / UAT / Prod, from config |
| `correlationId` | `string` | Yes | Pulled from `Activity.Current`/trace context if present, else generated |
| `actor` | `{id, name, type}` | Yes | Who/what performed the action |
| `action` | `string` | Yes | Event type/verb, e.g. `OrderCreated` |
| `entityType` / `entityId` | `string` | No | Resource affected |
| `status` | `enum: Success, Failure, Partial` | Yes | Outcome |
| `durationMs` | `long` | No | Optional timing |
| `details` | `IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>` | No | Free-form payload — subject to redaction, see below |

### 2. Public API
Split what the framework controls from what the caller can set, so the invariant fields structurally can't diverge between projects:

```csharp
public interface IAuditPublisher
{
    Task PublishAsync(AuditEventInput input, CancellationToken ct = default);
}
```

`AuditEventInput` should only expose `action`, `entity`, `actor`, `status`, `details` — everything else (`eventId`, `timestampUtc`, `schemaVersion`, `serviceName`, `environment`) is filled in by the framework itself.

### 3. Splunk transport
Deliver via Splunk HTTP Event Collector (HEC), JSON events, batched. Wrap each event in the standard HEC envelope.

### 4. Dependency injection
One-line registration, config-driven, no manual construction per project:
```csharp
services.AddSplunkAuditLogging(configuration);
```

### 5. Configuration
HEC URL, token, index, source/sourcetype, batch size/interval, and timeout all come from `appsettings.json` + environment-variable overrides. `serviceName`/`environment` are also config-bound, never passed by the caller. The HEC token itself should come from a secret provider (e.g. Key Vault), never plain JSON.

### 6. Correlation
Integrate with `System.Diagnostics.Activity` so `correlationId` is populated automatically from the current trace when the host app uses distributed tracing, with a fallback generator when it doesn't.

